Instance-loop prefetch depth in the fused GDN decode factory.

Past the core count the chunked decode factory gives each active core
`ceil(num_heads / ncores)` head-instances and the compute kernel runs them in a
sequential loop. The reader serves that loop one instance at a time, and each
iteration blocks until compute pops the previous instance, so every instance
pays its own read latency on top of compute.

This patch gives the reader-side circular buffers (q, k, v, beta, g, and the
initial state) a second instance's worth of room when a core carries more than
one instance. The reader can then reserve, read, and push instance i+1 while
compute still works on instance i, which hides the per-instance read latency
behind compute. With one instance per core the depth stays one, so the in-range
program mapping, memory layout, and numerics are unchanged and every receipt
produced under that mapping stays valid.

The change is a scheduling change: output and recurrent state must remain
bit-identical to the admitted kernel, which is what the matched physical screen
checks before any timing is read.

--- a/ttnn/cpp/ttnn/operations/transformer/gated_delta_decode/device/gated_delta_decode_program_factory.cpp
+++ b/ttnn/cpp/ttnn/operations/transformer/gated_delta_decode/device/gated_delta_decode_program_factory.cpp
@@ -47,12 +47,19 @@
         CreateCircularBuffer(program, cores, config);
     };
 
-    make_cb(0, key_tiles);    // q
-    make_cb(1, key_tiles);    // k
-    make_cb(2, val_tiles);    // v
-    make_cb(3, 1);            // beta
-    make_cb(4, 1);            // g
-    make_cb(5, state_tiles);  // state input
+    // Reader prefetch depth. When a core loops over several head-instances
+    // (heads_per_core > 1) the reader-side buffers carry a second instance's
+    // worth of room, so the reader streams instance i+1 while compute works on
+    // instance i and the per-instance read latency is hidden behind compute. With
+    // one instance per core the depth is one, so every in-range receipt keeps the
+    // same memory layout and the same numerics.
+    const uint32_t instance_buffers = (heads_per_core > 1) ? 2 : 1;
+    make_cb(0, key_tiles * instance_buffers);    // q
+    make_cb(1, key_tiles * instance_buffers);    // k
+    make_cb(2, val_tiles * instance_buffers);    // v
+    make_cb(3, instance_buffers);                // beta
+    make_cb(4, instance_buffers);                // g
+    make_cb(5, state_tiles * instance_buffers);  // state input
     make_cb(6, 1);            // exp(g)
     make_cb(7, state_tiles);  // decayed state
     make_cb(8, val_tiles);    // k @ state
